- ${GAMES}/last_call/, ${GAMES}/late_shifts/, ${GAMES}/the_inheritance/ toml_phases/*.toml — how each NPC's first-contact / on-ramp beat is authored (the auto-fire one-shot that sets <npc>_opened_up, the hub it opens). Which entrances are dramatized vs which are bare hub-spawns.

THE QUESTION: distill the reusable "proper entrance" TEMPLATE — pretext + name-on-the-page + hook-landed + the gate/flag mechanics that make it fire once then open the hub — and note where our own games FELL SHORT of it (bare spawns) so the doctrine forbids that.

Return the template as an ordered pattern + the shortfalls, with file:line citations.`,
    { label: 'npc-intro:our-games', phase: 'Study', schema: SWEEP }
  ),

  // 5 — corpus mining (recover craft, flag lies)
  () => agent(
`Read-only mining of the DEPRECATED corpus for recoverable craft on (a) player onboarding / first-session / tutorialization and (b) NPC introduction / first-contact. Same method we used for media.md: lift the craft, but FLAG anything that contradicts the live engine.

SEARCH: ${DJ}/prompts/ and ${DJ}/prompts_v2/ — grep for onboarding, opening, intro, first scene, tutorial, "first meeting", "introduce", cold open, first contact, day one, "new character". Read the relevant guide files in full (e.g. anything like *_writing_guide.md, toml_generation_prompt*, scene/structure guides).

THE QUESTION: what onboarding-method craft and NPC-introduction craft did the corpus carry that the current skill DROPPED and is worth recovering? AND: which corpus claims about HOW onboarding/intros work in the engine are FALSE (the corpus has a history of inventing engine facts — hardcoded arousal, fictional rent/phone keys)? Cross-check any engine claim against ${ENGINE} / ${IMPORT}.

Return: recoverable craft (with corpus path:line) + a list of corpus LIES to NOT re-inherit (each with the engine truth + citation).`,
    { label: 'corpus:mine', phase: 'Study', schema: SWEEP }
  ),

  // 6 — engine machinery (the ground truth the doctrine must bind to)
  () => agent(
`Read-only catalog of the REAL, code-verified engine machinery available for (a) onboarding a player and (b) introducing an NPC. The doctrine must ground ONLY in knobs that exist — this agent is the ground truth.

READ ${ENGINE} and ${IMPORT}. Catalog, with file:line and a one-line "what it gives you":
  ONBOARDING machinery: the starting_canvas / opening canvas chain (how the first passage + a linear opening sequence is wired); quest_cards + their tip/goal fields (the legible "what next" surface); the day-cycle router / sleep / power-down activity (how a day advances + resets resources); auto-fire capstone shape (priority, is_repeatable=false, single Continue — the scripted-beat primitive); the sidebar HUD trait bands + npc_panel (what the player can be shown); locked_text / show_when_locked Mode-A greyed-choice tooltip (the why-locked surface); blocked_message inline on a greyed nav card (the why-can't-go-there surface); costs={time,energy} travel friction.
  INTRODUCTION machinery: the first-contact one-shot (auto-fire, no npc/requires_npc, sets <npc>_opened_up); npc_at_location presence predicate; schedules (co-location) + how presence renders on nav; flag-gated capstone that fires once then opens a hub.

For EACH: confirm it exists (file:line), state exactly what it lets an author do, and note any trap (e.g. conditions need version="1.0" or fail-open; itemEffects camelCase; is_container swallows canvases). This list is what the proposed doctrine is allowed to use.`,
    { label: 'engine:machinery', phase: 'Study', schema: SWEEP }
  ),
])

phase('Synthesize')

const PROPOSAL = {
  type: "object",
  properties: {
    onboarding_doctrine: {
      type: "object",
      properties: {
        principle: { type: "string", description: "the one-line thesis for how onboarding should be done" },
        method: { type: "array", items: { type: "string" }, description: "the controlled-first-sequence / teaching pattern, as an ordered authorable procedure grounded in the evidence" },
        rubric: { type: "array", items: { type: "string" }, description: "checkable Step-6 acceptance items (verifiable, not vibe) — e.g. 'each live system is surfaced once in the opening'" },
        skill_placement: { type: "array", items: { type: "string" }, description: "exactly where this lands in the skill — new reference file? which existing files get one-line wires? (cite the audit's attach points)" },
        engine_grounding: { type: "array", items: { type: "string" }, description: "the real engine knobs it uses (from agent 6), each cited" },
      },
      required: ["principle", "method", "rubric", "skill_placement", "engine_grounding"],
    },
    npc_intro_doctrine: {
      type: "object",
      properties: {
        principle: { type: "string" },
        method: { type: "array", items: { type: "string" }, description: "the 'proper entrance' pattern as an ordered authorable procedure (pretext + name-on-page + hook + fire-once-then-open), grounded in Renner + the best-game patterns" },
        rubric: { type: "array", items: { type: "string" } },
        skill_placement: { type: "array", items: { type: "string" } },
        engine_grounding: { type: "array", items: { type: "string" } },
      },
      required: ["principle", "method", "rubric", "skill_placement", "engine_grounding"],
    },
    corpus_lies_to_avoid: { type: "array", items: { type: "string" }, description: "false engine facts from the corpus to explicitly NOT re-inherit, each with the truth" },
    vesper_dogfood: { type: "array", items: { type: "string" }, description: "how each doctrine would concretely apply to Vesper as the dogfood proof (the machine-onboarding fix to the opening; Renner-as-template + the remaining-cast checklist)" },
    open_questions: { type: "array", items: { type: "string" }, description: "the genuine forks for LO to decide — scope/shape choices, not trivia" },
  },
  required: ["onboarding_doctrine", "npc_intro_doctrine", "vesper_dogfood", "open_questions"],
}

const proposal = await agent(
`You are synthesizing a PROPOSED doctrine shape (NOT writing it into any file) for two recurring author-game skill gaps: player-onboarding (the machine-teaching half) and NPC-introduction (the dramatic-entrance half). LO's directive: don't randomly add rules — first establish how it should be done PROPERLY, grounded in how the best games + our own wins actually do it, triaged against the engine.

${AUDIT_CONTEXT}

SWEEP EVIDENCE (six read-only studies — best external games, our games, corpus, engine — for both sub-problems):
${JSON.stringify(sweep, null, 2)}

Produce the proposed doctrine shape per the schema. Requirements:
- Ground EVERY method step in observed evidence (cite the game/corpus/engine source) — no invented patterns, no invented engine knobs. If the evidence is thin somewhere, say so rather than inventing.
- The onboarding doctrine must teach a METHOD (an authorable controlled-first-sequence / diegetic-teaching procedure) and a CHECKABLE rubric — fixing the audit's core gap ("a goal with no how").
- The NPC-intro doctrine must elevate the first encounter to a designed beat: pretext (why the meeting happens) + name-on-the-page + land-the-hook, then the fire-once-open-the-hub mechanics — codifying Renner as the template without losing the existing mechanical on-ramp doctrine.
- Both must keep the skill's register (RTS-flat, render-don't-dump, no tutorial popups) — the teaching is diegetic, in-fiction, by play.
- skill_placement must say concretely whether each is a NEW reference file vs edits to existing files, and name the files (use the audit's cited attach points: step-2 §8, content-framework §1E, step-5 Pass-4, beat-authoring, lanes.md npc_intro, step-3 casting).
- vesper_dogfood: concrete, what we'd actually change in Vesper to prove each.
- open_questions: only genuine forks worth LO's call (e.g. one consolidated onboarding reference vs distributed edits; how heavy a scripted first sequence should be vs staying fully-open-sandbox).`,
  { label: 'synthesize:proposal', phase: 'Synthesize', schema: PROPOSAL, effort: 'high' }
)

phase('Critique')

const CRITIQUE = {
  type: "object",
  properties: {
    grounded: { type: "boolean", description: "is every method step + engine knob backed by the sweep evidence?" },
    invented_or_unbacked: { type: "array", items: { type: "string" }, description: "any claim/knob/pattern NOT backed by a citation in the evidence — the things to cut or re-ground" },
    overfit_risks: { type: "array", items: { type: "string" }, description: "anything overfit to RTS / Vesper / DoL that wouldn't generalize across games" },
    register_conflicts: { type: "array", items: { type: "string" }, description: "anywhere the proposal risks importing a tutorial-dump / non-RTS-flat instinct against the skill's register" },
    coverage_gaps: { type: "array", items: { type: "string" }, description: "what the proposal MISSED — a sub-question, a system, an NPC-arrival shape, a checkable item" },
    verdict: { type: "string", description: "is this proposal ready to take to LO as 'how it should be done properly', or does it need another pass — and on what" },
  },
  required: ["grounded", "invented_or_unbacked", "overfit_risks", "coverage_gaps", "verdict"],
}

const critique = await agent(
`Adversarially review this PROPOSED onboarding + NPC-introduction doctrine shape. Your job is to REFUTE, not rubber-stamp. Default to skeptical.

THE PROPOSAL:
${JSON.stringify(proposal, null, 2)}

THE EVIDENCE IT CLAIMS TO REST ON:
${JSON.stringify(sweep, null, 2)}

Check hard:
1. GROUNDED? Every method step and every engine knob must trace to a citation in the evidence. List anything invented or unbacked — especially any engine knob not confirmed by the engine-machinery study (the corpus has a history of inventing engine facts; don't let one slip in).
2. OVERFIT? Flag anything that only works for RTS / Vesper / DoL and wouldn't generalize to a different sandbox-adult game.
3. REGISTER? Flag anywhere the proposal sneaks in a tutorial-dump, a popup, or a non-diegetic teaching method against the skill's RTS-flat / render-don't-dump law.
4. COVERAGE? What did it MISS — a sub-question from LO's concern (teach nav / economy / day-cycle / live-systems / win-fail; the per-NPC entrance for mid/late arrivals), a checkable rubric item, an entrance shape?
5. VERDICT: ready for LO, or needs a specific second pass.

Be concrete and cite. A short, sharp refutation list beats a long endorsement.`,
  { label: 'critique:adversarial', phase: 'Critique', schema: CRITIQUE, effort: 'high' }
)

return { proposal, critique }

